  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nRest of Today into Tonight... Broad synoptic lift associated \nwith an upper low will continue to provide for clouds and light \nrain through this evening. The cool temperatures and rain could\nallow for some fog/mist to persist in areas where it rains, after\nthe rain comes to an end. As this moves out, surface high \npressure over the Great Lakes will start to take over and we \nwill clear out. How much we clear out will have a big impact on \ntemperatures. With cloudy skies during the day we have not been\nwarming much at all. Where we can clear out tonight will be \nable to cool more due to radiational cooling. The best chances \nfor clearing will be across Wisconsin and parts of mainly \neastern Minnesota tonight. Depending on how quickly this \nclearing can occur will impact how cold it gets. If clearing \noccurs quickly temperatures could fall into the upper 30s and \nsome frost could form. Best chance for this will be from Mille \nLacs in Minnesota over to Rusk in Wisconsin, with the main cold \nspot being in NE MN and NW WI covered by NWS DLH.\n\nTuesday to Friday... With the upper low moving out and high\npressure remaining nearby we should see much more sun on\nTuesday. Temperatures will still be below normal in the lower\n60s as the airmass doesn't change much. Wednesday and Thursday \nis when we will see a change in pattern and a shift in the \nweather. Southerly winds at 850 mb will bring some WAA. A 500 \nmb shortwave is expected across the Dakotas and a jet streak \nacross the Northern Plain/Upper Midwest will provide some lift.\nAll of this together will provide support for out next rain \nchance. Best chance for rain will be off to our west across the \nDakota and into western Minnesota. Chance for rain will fall off\nto the east across Minnesota. The warmer air advecting in \nshould allow for some increase in temperature though in to the \nmid 60s. By mid day Thursday ensemble chances drop quickly \nacross the board. Temperatures similar to maybe slightly cooler \non Thursday thanks to expected cloud cover. Friday will see a \nsmall chance for some additional rain across western and \nsouthern Minnesota associated with a small 500 mb disturbance. \nOverall very scattered ensemble signal and low confidence in \nthis rain occurring. \n\nNext weekend... Temperatures go into a warming trend this\nweekend with Sunday being our next chance for temperatures above\nnormal. Sunday is forecast to see temperatures in the upper 60s\nwith some chances for the low 70s. Normals today have highs in\nthe lower 70s and by Sunday they fall into the upper 60s.\nSaturday will also see another 500 mb disturbance across the \nDakotas, so another chance for rain with the better chances in \nwestern Minnesota similar to Wednesday/Thursday. Although this \none does not fall off as fast as the chance earlier in the week,\nso chances to the east are not as low.",
